A `Channel` is used inside an arrow here: an arrow is lifted into a function of its own that the channel rules do not follow, so a channel is declared, sent on and received from only in a top-level function's own body; write the arrow as a module function, `const relayBody = (q: Pipe): i32 => { q.ch.send(1); return 1; }`, and pass `relayBody` where the arrow was
